Companies such as Google, XOCEAN, and Wayflyer must follow national health and safety laws. In Ireland, the main legislation is the Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005.

1. Legal Requirements (Apply to All Companies)

Under this law, all employers are required to:

Conduct risk assessments to identify workplace hazards
Prepare a written Safety Statement
Provide a safe working environment and equipment
Offer training, supervision, and clear instructions
Report and investigate accidents and incidents
Provide personal protective equipment (PPE) where necessary

These requirements apply equally to all companies, regardless of industry.

2. Company-Specific Health and Safety Practices
Google

Google operates mainly in office and technical environments.

Key focus areas:

Ergonomics (safe workstation setup)
Fire and electrical safety
Data centre safety procedures
Employee wellbeing and mental health support

Risk level: Low to medium

XOCEAN
XOCEAN operates in offshore marine environments, which involve higher physical risks.

Key focus areas:

Marine and operational safety procedures
Detailed risk assessments and hazard control
Emergency response planning
Environmental protection measures
Strict equipment safety protocols

Risk level: High

Wayflyer

Wayflyer is an office-based fintech company.

Key focus areas:
Office safety (fire, equipment, workspace)
Remote working safety
Employee wellbeing and mental health
Compliance with workplace regulations

Risk level: Low

